[38;2;124;124;124mnorm similar to Euclidean space (i.e., $\ell^2$) and the space of square [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mintegrable functions ($L^2$) are isometrically isomorphic.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe two spaces are:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. $\ell^2$ — the space of square-summable sequences $(x_n)$ with norm $\|x\| = [0m[38;2;124;124;124m(\sum |x_n|^2)^{1/2}$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. $L^2$ — the space of square-integrable functions (presumably $L^2[0,1]$ or [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$L^2(\mathbb{R})$, but typically $L^2[0,1]$ is the standard one considered).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question: are they isometrically isomorphic?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFirst, they are isomorphic as Hilbert spaces (both are separable infinite-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdimensional Hilbert spaces, so they're unitarily equivalent). But isometrically [0m[38;2;124;124;124misomorphic?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAn isometric isomorphism between normed spaces is a linear bijection that [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpreserves the norm. For Hilbert spaces, a linear isometry preserves the inner [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mproduct (by the polarization identity). So an isometric isomorphism between $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\ell^2$ and $L^2$ would be a unitary equivalence.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, every separable infinite-dimensional Hilbert space is unitarily [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mequivalent to $\ell^2$. This is a classical theorem. The key fact: any two [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mseparable infinite-dimensional Hilbert spaces are unitarily equivalent (hence [0m
[38;2;124;124;124misometrically isomorphic).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe proof: Take an orthonormal basis $\{e_n\}$ of $L^2[0,1]$ (e.g., the Fourier [0m[38;2;124;124;124mbasis or Haar basis). Then the map $T: \ell^2 \to L^2[0,1]$ defined by $T((a_n))[0m[38;2;124;124;124m= \sum a_n e_n$ is a unitary (hence isometric) isomorphism.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the answer is YES, they are isometrically isomorphic.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write a complete proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe key steps:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. Both $\ell^2$ and $L^2[0,1]$ are separable infinite-dimensional Hilbert [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mspaces.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. Every separable infinite-dimensional Hilbert space has a countable [0m
[38;2;124;124;124morthonormal basis.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m3. Given orthonormal bases $\{e_n\}$ of $H_1$ and $\{f_n\}$ of $H_2$, the map [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msending $e_n \mapsto f_n$ extends to a unitary (isometric isomorphism).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me be careful about what "$L^2$" means. The problem says "square integrable [0m[38;2;124;124;124mfunctions." This likely means $L^2[0,1]$ or $L^2(\mathbb{R})$. If [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$L^2(\mathbb{R})$, it's still separable and infinite-dimensional, so the same [0m
[38;2;124;124;124margument applies. Let me consider the general case.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, $L^2(\mathbb{R})$ is also separable (since $\mathbb{R}$ is $\sigma$-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcompact and $L^2$ on a $\sigma$-finite measure space with separable $L^2$... [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwell, $L^2(\mathbb{R})$ is separable because step functions with rational [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcoefficients on rational intervals are dense). So both are separable infinite-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdimensional Hilbert spaces.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe theorem: Any two separable infinite-dimensional Hilbert spaces are [0m
[38;2;124;124;124misometrically isomorphic.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mProof of the theorem:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Let $H_1, H_2$ be separable infinite-dimensional Hilbert spaces.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Each has a countable orthonormal basis: $\{e_n\}_{n=1}^\infty$ for $H_1$, $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\{f_n\}_{n=1}^\infty$ for $H_2$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Define $U: H_1 \to H_2$ by $U(\sum a_n e_n) = \sum a_n f_n$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- This is well-defined, linear, bijective, and $\|Ux\| = \|x\|$ (Parseval's [0m
[38;2;124;124;124midentity on both sides).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Hence $U$ is an isometric isomorphism.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow I need to verify:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. $\ell^2$ is a separable infinite-dimensional Hilbert space. ✓ (standard)[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. $L^2$ (of the relevant measure space) is a separable infinite-dimensional [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mHilbert space. ✓[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $L^2[0,1]$: The standard orthonormal basis is $\{e^{2\pi i n x}\}_{n \in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbb{Z}}$ (Fourier basis), which is countable. So $L^2[0,1]$ has a countable [0m[38;2;124;124;124mONB, hence is separable. It's infinite-dimensional (contains all polynomials, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhich are linearly independent).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $L^2(\mathbb{R})$: The Hermite functions form a countable ONB. So it's also [0m[38;2;124;124;124mseparable and infinite-dimensional.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the answer is YES.[0m

